From: canoziia <canoziia@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] deepseek-v4-flash: uniform verification graphs for larger PP
 cohorts

VLLM_DSV4_VERIFICATION_COHORTS (default 0 = unchanged). With
VLLM_DSV41_VERIFICATION the CPU-known uniform verification graphs exist for
1-8, 16 and 32 requests per width. At max-num-seqs 32 over PP3 the live-load
cohorts hold ~9-11 requests, so an 11-request width-4 step (44 rows) ran the
16-request 64-row graph: +45 % verified rows, and the K=5 engine fell behind a
K=3 engine at c32 (101.8 vs ~78 ms per step at width 3). The switch also
captures 12 and 24 requests per width (10 more FULL graphs at K=5; every 2/4
requests up to 32 did not fit in the tightest rank's memory). With it and the
re-swept cost table, c32 at width 3 costs 84 ms per step (K=3 engine ~78) and
width 4 no longer misses the graphs (97.9 instead of 144 ms).

---
diff --git a/vllm/v1/worker/gpu/cudagraph_utils.py b/vllm/v1/worker/gpu/cudagraph_utils.py
--- a/vllm/v1/worker/gpu/cudagraph_utils.py
+++ b/vllm/v1/worker/gpu/cudagraph_utils.py
@@ -489,6 +489,15 @@
             # cohorts used by PP6, and powers of two for larger bursts.
             req_counts = sorted(set(range(1, min(8, self.max_num_reqs) + 1))
                                 | {n for n in (16, 32) if n <= self.max_num_reqs})
+            if os.environ.get("VLLM_DSV4_VERIFICATION_COHORTS", "0") == "1":
+                # PP cohorts of 9..32 requests (max-num-seqs 32 over PP3 gives
+                # ~11) otherwise pad to the 16/32-request uniform graph: an
+                # 11-request width-4 step ran the 64-row graph (+45 % rows).
+                # 12 and 24 requests as well: every extra FULL graph costs
+                # memory on the tightest PP rank (all of 10..32 in steps of
+                # 2/4 failed to capture there).
+                req_counts = sorted(set(req_counts) | {
+                    n for n in (12, 24) if n <= self.max_num_reqs})
             for reqs, width, loras in product(
                 req_counts, range(1, self.decode_query_len + 1),
                 self.lora_capture_cases,
